/**
 * @file strings.js
 * @description Every word of the configuration workbook, in the three languages
 * an engagement can be run in.
 *
 * The workbook is handed to the client's finance and logistics teams to fill in,
 * which makes it as client-facing as the proposal — and it was the last thing the
 * tool produced that was English whatever language the engagement was run in. It
 * is not a prompt, so there was no instruction to add: it is a Markdown template
 * generated by code, and the only honest fix is the words themselves.
 *
 * Three rules held throughout, the same ones the prompts follow:
 *
 *   Shopify's own names stay as they are, including the admin paths a client has
 *   to find on their own screen — translating "Settings > Taxes and duties" would
 *   send them looking for a menu that does not exist in their admin unless their
 *   admin is in that language too, and we do not know that it is.
 *
 *   Customs, tax and standards names are other authorities' proper nouns: IOSS,
 *   Peppol, XRechnung, SdI, KSeF, DDP, DAP, HS codes.
 *
 *   Everything a person reads as a sentence is written in their language, not
 *   carried across word by word.
 *
 * English is the source and is what an engagement with no language recorded gets.
 *
 * @module workbook/strings
 */

import { isEnglish } from '../shared/language.js';

